---
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import SubNav from "../../../components/SubNav.astro";
import Section from "../../../components/Section.astro";
import Card from "../../../components/Card.astro";

const instanceState = `{
  "instance_id": "01J9...",          // one live copy
  "blueprint_version": "crypt-03@4", // pinned; the master keeps moving
  "seed": 18446744073709551615,      // nullable: authored maps have none
  "born_at": "2026-10-27T17:40:00Z",
  "expires_at": "2026-10-27T18:40:00Z",
  "status": "live",                  // live -> closing -> closed
  "state": {
    "taken":        ["chest-12", "chest-19"],
    "opened":       ["door-east"],
    "cleared":      ["boss-03"],
    "portal":       "open"
  }
}`;

const blueprint = `{
  "id": "crypt-03",
  "kind": "hybrid",                  // procedural | authored | hybrid
  "generator": "stacked-rooms@1",
  "params": { "depth": 3, "level_band": [4, 7], "biome": "crypt" },
  "template": {                       // authored parts, reused verbatim
    "entrance": "crypt-mouth-a",
    "boss_room": "ossuary-b",
    "modules": ["well-shaft", "collapsed-hall"]
  },
  "author": null,                     // a player for hand-built dungeons
  "reusable": true
}`;
---

<Base title="Dungeon data & schema — Party Dungeon" current="/mechanics/dungeon/">
  <SubNav current="/mechanics/dungeon/" />

  <header>
    <div class="kicker">
      <a href="/mechanics/">Mechanics</a> /
      <a href="/mechanics/dungeon/">Your dungeon</a> / Data &amp; schema
    </div>
    <h1>A dungeon is a blueprint, a clock, and a list of what's gone.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Generated maps were never the hard part &mdash; a seed could rebuild one.
      A dungeon with a timed life and loot already taken can't be rebuilt from
      anything but itself.
    </p>
  </header>

  <Section title="Three things we were calling a dungeon">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="The blueprint" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          What the map <em>is</em>: a generator call, a hand-built layout, or a
          mix. Reusable, versioned, never mutates in play.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="The instance" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          One live copy, with a <strong>timed life</strong>. It changes as
          people play in it, and those changes have to outlive the process
          that served them.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="The base" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          A player's own dungeon: an authored blueprint that never expires,
          because it's the place you come home to.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="bet" title="Revised: a seed is not enough">
        <p>
          The first pass of this page said a run map could be stored as a seed.
          That held only while the map was read-only. With a clock and loot
          that stays taken, the instance is the record.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Blueprints come from three places">
    <pre style="margin-bottom:16px"><code>{blueprint}</code></pre>
    <div class="split">
      <Card status="decided" title="Procedural" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Seed plus generator plus params. Rebuilding it is cheap and exact, so
          the blueprint stores the call, not the geometry.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Authored" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Hand-built, start to finish. It cannot be regenerated at all, so the
          layout <strong>is</strong> stored &mdash; and can be reused to spawn
          many instances of the same place.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Hybrid, and reuse" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Hand-authored pieces &mdash; an entrance, a boss room, a set of
          modules &mdash; stitched together by the generator. The same authored
          room can appear in a hundred different maps.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A blueprint is a row, a layout is a document">
        <p>
          One table of blueprints, each holding its authored parts as
          <code>jsonb</code>. Reference them by id from any instance; edit one
          and every future instance changes, which is exactly what reuse means.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Revised: authored first" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          The first build ships hand-made dungeons only. Procedural and hybrid
          stay in the model because a placement list is what a generator would
          emit anyway &mdash; but nothing depends on one existing yet.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="An instance is a delta">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      Rebuild the static world from the blueprint, then replay what happened to
      it. The expensive half is cheap again; only the changes are stored.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{instanceState}</code></pre>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Store what stays true after a restart">
        <p>
          Looted containers, opened doors, dead bosses, portal state. Not
          players, not mobs, not physics &mdash; those live in memory and die
          with the process, which is the correct outcome.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Checkpoint on events, not on a tick">
        <p>
          A chest opens, a boss dies, a door unlocks: write then, plus a slow
          heartbeat. A crash costs the last few seconds of play, never the
          whole run.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Claims are idempotent">
        <p>
          Loot is claimed by id. If a crash rolls the delta back, the same
          chest cannot pay out twice to two different parties.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The instance is the join point">
        <p>
          Runs, participants, and loot all point at the instance. The seed is
          one nullable column on its blueprint, not the identity of anything.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="The clock">
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="Every instance expires" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Dungeons have a timed life. <code>expires_at</code> is set when the
          instance is born and survives restarts, because it's a timestamp, not
          a running timer.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Expiry closes the door, not the run" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          When the clock runs out, <strong>nobody new gets in</strong>. Everyone
          already inside stays until they choose to leave &mdash; no ejection,
          no forfeited loot. The instance ends when the last of them walks out.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The reaper is a query, and it waits">
        <p>
          <code>expires_at &lt; now()</code> on a live instance flips it to
          <code>closing</code>, not <code>closed</code>. It only closes when it
          has no participants left, so the state machine is three values and
          no timers.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="One door check, no special cases">
        <p>
          Entry is allowed exactly while <code>status = 'live'</code>. Expiry
          and exclusivity both become the same guard, instead of two rules that
          can disagree under load.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Assets: payload in a bucket, truth in the database">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      GLB files live in object storage. The database holds the collision shape
      and everything the server needs to simulate an object without ever
      downloading its pixels.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="GLB in object storage, metadata in the database" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          The file is the render payload. The row is the authoritative
          description: identity, bounds, collision, counts, material slots.
          Two stores, one asset.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Collision is stored, not read from the GLB" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          The server has no renderer. Deriving hulls from a GLB at load would
          mean pulling megabytes over the wire to move one mob, so the shape is
          extracted once and kept in the row.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="decided" title="Nothing is ever deleted" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Rows still <strong>retire</strong> (<code>retired_at</code>,
          <code>superseded_by</code>) rather than vanish. Whether the bytes in
          the bucket are equally immortal is now a separate question, because
          they cost actual money.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Content-addressed by the file hash">
        <p>
          <code>sha256</code> of the GLB is the object key and the row's
          identity. Two players uploading the same crate store one file, and the
          client can cache forever because those bytes never change.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The server never fetches a GLB">
        <p>
          Authority runs on hulls and metadata, so the bucket can be a public
          CDN origin the game server has no credentials for. It also means the
          server keeps running when the asset host is down.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="One file per object, LODs as extra keys">
        <p>
          Textures stay inside the GLB, so loading an object is one GET. A row
          per texture is a filesystem rebuilt in SQL, and it makes every fetch a
          graph traversal. LODs and variants are separate keys under the same
          asset row.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Bounds cull, hulls collide">
        <p>
          Broadphase stays an AABB/OBB check, which is free. Narrowphase uses a
          few convex hulls per prefab, quantized to the bounding box so the whole
          proxy fits in a couple of kilobytes.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="The proxy wins arguments">
        <p>
          Where the GLB and the stored hull disagree, movement follows the hull.
          A player will occasionally clip a visible edge or walk through a gap
          that looks solid. That is the price of an authoritative shape that
          isn't the render mesh.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="One shape, both sides">
        <p>
          Client and server collide against the same stored hulls. Anything else
          and the server's idea of where a player can stand drifts from what
          they see, which is the bug nobody can reproduce.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Validation happens at bake">
        <p>
          The inputs are ours, so the hostile-binary problem is gone. What's left
          is geometry: manifold inputs, a vertex budget after decimation, and
          hull extraction that produces something an agent can actually walk on.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Materials survive as slots">
        <p>
          Textures ride inside the GLB, but the row keeps the material slot
          names. That lets a blueprint recolour a crate or swap a wall's surface
          without re-exporting a model for every variant.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="bet" title="Revised: forever stopped being affordable">
        <p>
          The previous pass said keeping everything forever was a rounding error
          because there were no render bytes. A 20&nbsp;MB GLB kept forever is a
          bill. Metadata is still cheap; the payload is the part with a price.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="New meshes are recipes, not uploads">
    <p class="lede" style="font-size:16px; margin-bottom:18px">
      Nobody uploads a model. Players combine the ones we ship &mdash; union,
      subtract, intersect, then change the surface &mdash; and the result is a
      recipe with a baked mesh behind it.
    </p>
    <div class="grid">
      <Card status="decided" title="The library is the vocabulary" tag="Yours">
        <p>
          Every new mesh is made of meshes we made. Moderation stops being a
          queue of strangers' files and becomes a property of the starting kit:
          if it is in the library, it has already been looked at.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="A derived asset is a recipe plus a bake">
        <p>
          <code>derived_from</code>, the operations, their transforms, material
          overrides. The recipe is the identity; the baked GLB is the payload,
          cached under that identity. Both immutable, both kept.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Booleans need watertight inputs">
        <p>
          CSG on a mesh with holes either fails or produces new holes, and the
          hole is discovered three boolean steps later. The library has to be
          manifold as a hard constraint on how we author it, not a nice-to-have.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Booleans are lossy, so bake once">
        <p>
          Every cut shreds topology and multiplies vertices. Decimate to a budget
          at bake time, never at load, and never re-run a chain at runtime &mdash;
          the result would drift between sessions.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Texture changes are material overrides">
        <p>
          No new image files. Swap a slot, tint it, remap the UVs through the
          material. Variants then share geometry and the bucket stays small,
          which is also why the texture step is the cheap one.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="One canonical bake, server-side">
        <p>
          Floating-point booleans do not agree across machines, so one bake
          produces the GLB everyone loads. The editor previews locally and
          publishes when the server's bake lands; a preview is never the truth.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Provenance is the derivation graph">
        <p>
          Who built what out of whose work is an edge in the graph, permanently.
          That is exactly what a payout rule needs, and the reason "nothing is
          ever deleted" stops being sentiment and starts being bookkeeping.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Database shape">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li><b>accounts</b> &mdash; one per player. Owns characters, dungeons, items.</li>
        <li><b>characters</b> &mdash; class, level, stats. The thing a run puts at risk.</li>
        <li><b>parties</b> &mdash; the assembled roster. Shape still open, see below.</li>
        <li><b>dungeons</b> &mdash; a player's base: owner, layout, door policy. Never expires.</li>
        <li><b>blueprints</b> &mdash; procedural params, authored parts, author, reusable flag.</li>
        <li><b>instances</b> &mdash; blueprint, optional seed, state <code>jsonb</code>, born/expires, status: live, closing, closed.</li>
        <li><b>instance_participants</b> &mdash; who entered, when they left. "No participants" is what closes an instance.</li>
        <li><b>dungeon_visits</b> &mdash; traffic through a player's base. The point of opening the doors.</li>
        <li><b>items</b> &mdash; instances: definition, level, runes, stats, crafted_by, location.</li>
        <li><b>item_definitions</b>, <b>runes</b> &mdash; the crafting catalogue, data not code.</li>
        <li><b>assets</b> &mdash; identity, bounds, collision hulls, counts, material slots, moderation, retired_at. No render bytes.</li>
        <li><b>asset_objects</b> &mdash; object-store key (the sha256), byte size, mime, LOD, storage class.</li>
        <li><b>asset_recipes</b> &mdash; derived_from, boolean ops, transforms, material overrides, bake version.</li>
        <li><b>materials</b> &mdash; named slots a blueprint can override: tint, swap, remap. Textures live in the GLB.</li>
        <li><b>prefabs</b> &mdash; GLB object key, stored collision, scale. Blocks add behaviour and placement rules on top.</li>
        <li><b>runs</b> &mdash; party, instance, outcome. No seed here any more.</li>
        <li><b>listings</b> &mdash; market rows: item, seller, price, state.</li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
    <div class="grid" style="margin-top:16px">
      <Card status="guess" title="Postgres, and jsonb where it will churn">
        <p>
          Rust and <code>sqlx</code>. Blueprint templates, instance state, and
          item stats are <code>jsonb</code> until their shape stops moving;
          anything joined or filtered on gets a real column.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Items are immutable; ownership moves">
        <p>
          Runes and level are fixed at craft time. Selling or stashing rewrites
          <code>owner</code> and <code>location</code> only, never the item's
          identity.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Instances archive, they don't vanish">
        <p>
          Expired state is kept, not deleted: it is the only witness to what a
          party claims it found. Cheap rows, and disputes stop being he-said.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card status="guess" title="Bases and instances stay separate tables">
        <p>
          A player's dungeon is permanent and owned; an instance is temporary
          and shared. One table with a nullable clock would invite queries that
          forget which is which.
        </p>
      </Card>
      <Card href="/mechanics/dungeon/schema/" status="guess" title="This, as a migration">
        <p>
          Every table above, plus the constraints, triggers and partial indexes
          that make the rules in this list impossible to break by accident.
        </p>
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section title="Open questions">
    <Card>
      <ul class="plain">
        <li>
          <b>What if nobody ever leaves?</b> A closing instance waits for its
          last participant, and a disconnected or AFK player is still a
          participant. Force-close after a grace period, or on disconnect
          timeout &mdash; the schema needs an answer before a dungeon can hang
          open forever.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Does the clock run while the dungeon is empty?</b> An instance can
          expire with nobody inside, which closes it immediately. Pausing the
          clock when empty is kinder, and costs a <code>time_spent</code>
          column.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Is a party one player's roster, or four players?</b> Decides
          whether <code>parties</code> points at an account or a join table.
          Everything downstream moves with it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Who authors a reusable dungeon, and who gets paid?</b> If players
          can hand-build maps others run, the blueprint needs an owner and a
          payout rule.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>What is one dungeon's asset budget?</b> Not just storage now &mdash;
          it is the download a visitor waits through at the door. Without a cap,
          the first ambitious author sets the ceiling for everyone.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>How small is "very small"?</b> A collision proxy of 32 points per
          prefab and one of 2,000 are both "small" next to a render mesh and
          behave very differently on a ramp. The budget is a gameplay number,
          not a storage one.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Are blobs immortal too?</b> Rows never die, but a 20&nbsp;MB GLB
          kept for a deleted account and an abandoned blueprint is a bill that
          grows forever. Either "nothing is deleted" means the bytes as well, or
          it means the record survives and the payload may not.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>How deep can a derivation go?</b> Two booleans keep meshes clean;
          ten turn them into gravel. A depth cap is also a licence to stop
          supporting old bakes, so it is a policy about the past, not just about
          triangles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Do visitors' changes persist in someone's base?</b> A visitor
          looting a chest in your open dungeon is either a permanent loss or a
          reset on exit. Both are defensible; only one is cheap.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>What happens to a run when the server dies?</b> Restore from the
          last checkpoint, roll the run back, or keep nothing? This is the
          player-facing one.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </Card>
  </Section>
</Base>
